WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.700
The bridge is clean.

2
00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:02.840
Then the tickets start.

3
00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.740
A thin, high whine on every call.

4
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:07.200
The meter says exactly
four thousand hertz, the limit.

5
00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.900
A cheap clock chip leaks
its tick into your samples.

6
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.840
Can a few lines of code remove
the whine and leave the voice alone?

7
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.260
Try the laziest idea.

8
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.300
Average each sample
with the one before it.

9
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.020
The whine's samples flip,
up, down, up, down,

10
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:20.860
so every average is zero. Gone.

11
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.480
A slow voice tone, five hundred hertz,
comes out a touch smaller,

12
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.520
half a sample late.

13
00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:25.790
Why?

14
00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.710
Lesson three's wheel.

15
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.490
Put every tone on one wheel at once,

16
00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:30.810
at the spot its dot
reaches after one sample.

17
00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:31.850
That's the unit circle.

18
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.170
Zero hertz at the right,
two thousand at the top,

19
00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:35.290
four thousand at the left.

20
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.290
The whine flips,
so it sits at the far left.

21
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.550
There the average's output is zero,

22
00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.010
so the point is called a zero.

23
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.050
How much of a tone passes
is its distance to the zero over two,

24
00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:45.450
the distance of zero hertz.

25
00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.450
Two thousand hertz
is one point four one away,

26
00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:49.070
so seventy-one percent passes.

27
00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.410
The whine sits on the zero.

28
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.290
Nothing passes.

29
00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.210
Then the chip is swapped, and the whine
comes back at two thousand hertz,

30
00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.130
at the top.

31
00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.850
A real tone is two half-size
arrows turning opposite ways,

32
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.650
the mirror arrow on the bottom half.

33
00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.110
So it needs a zero pair.

34
00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.050
Average each sample with the one two back,
and it's gone again.

35
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:06.680
Next ticket.

36
00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.000
Everyone sounds like they're
talking through a sock.

37
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.860
Zeros weaken their neighbors too.

38
00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.860
Fifteen hundred hertz
is zero point three nine

39
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:15.020
and one point nine six from the zeros.

40
00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.980
That's zero point seven seven,
against two for zero hertz.

41
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:19.000
Just thirty-eight percent.

42
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.140
So let the filter listen to itself,
reusing its own output, two samples back.

43
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.420
That feedback puts poles just
inside the rim, beside the zeros.

44
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:29.380
Divide by the distance to each pole.

45
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.760
From fifteen hundred hertz,

46
00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:33.300
the zero and the pole look equally far,
so they cancel.

47
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.360
Ninety-seven percent.

48
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.920
The whine still sits on its zero.

49
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.940
That's a notch.

50
00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.050
Move the poles out
from the center toward the rim,

51
00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:41.570
and the notch gets narrower.

52
00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:42.710
At zero point nine,

53
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.690
nineteen hundred hertz
keeps sixty percent.

54
00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:46.890
At zero point nine eight,
it keeps ninety-seven.

55
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:48.930
So why not put the poles on the rim?

56
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.960
Send the feedback loop one click.

57
00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.300
Every sample, its echo keeps
zero point nine of itself,

58
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.420
the pole's radius.

59
00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:57.220
Inside the circle, the echo dies out.

60
00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.660
On the rim, it rings forever.

61
00:01:58.700 --> 00:01:59.720
Outside, it blows up.

62
00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.760
A filter with every pole inside is stable.

63
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:04.460
The filter never hunts for the whine.

64
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.860
It treats every tone
by its point on the circle,

65
00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:08.740
even a sung two thousand hertz note.

66
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.120
Then a new ticket.

67
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.620
Every time a call starts,
a faint ring at two thousand hertz

68
00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.060
is left behind, like a bell.
